This project stems from a peculiar ritual I have: regardless of portion size, I always leave a last bite on my plate. This inspired me to make a book exploring this phenomenon across cultures, weighing its benefits and drawbacks.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 I designed the text as an edible language: sentences are bites, full stops are brief pauses, and large paragraph gaps are deep breaths.\cb1 \
\cb3 Custom glyphs are derived from silhouettes of my own leftover pieces. By pairing red, which stimulates appetite, with blue, which suppresses it, the colour palette reinforces the theme.\cb1 \
\cb3 As the book progresses, the layout gradually opens up, revealing more negative space as if the words themselves were being eaten away.\cb1 \
\cb3 Each chapter has its own visual language: the meal-planning section is neatly divided into clear, bite-sized blocks, while the global-impact pages spill into chaotic, disordered spreads.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 The book opens and closes with tinfoil pages, like wrapped-up leftovers, framing the experience.}